Strategy Pattern: Allows you to choose an algorithm at runtime.
 Example: A payment gateway system that selects between PayPal, Stripe, or Credit

This is extremely useful in backend applications.

Problem

Suppose you have different payment methods:
Credit Card
PayPal
UPI

You don't want a huge if-else.

Strategy interface
interface PaymentStrategy {
    void pay(double amount);
}

Credit card:

class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid using Credit Card: " + amount);
    }
}

UPI:

class UPIPayment implements PaymentStrategy {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid using UPI: " + amount);
    }
}
Payment service
class PaymentService {

    private PaymentStrategy strategy;

    PaymentService(PaymentStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    public void makePayment(double amount) {
        strategy.pay(amount);
    }
}

Now:

PaymentService service =
        new PaymentService(new UPIPayment());

service.makePayment(1000);

Output:

Paid using UPI: 1000

Tomorrow you can change:

new CreditCardPayment()

without changing PaymentService.

Strategy = Choose an algorithm/behavior at runtime.
Strategy is a behavioral design pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each algorithm separately, and allows the appropriate algorithm to be
selected at runtime.
